    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/texas">Texas</a> declared independence from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> on 2 March 1836, after Santa Anna's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siete-leyes">Siete Leyes</a> centralised the Mexican republic and he began enforcing laws against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a>, which Mexico had abolished in 1829 and which the Texians refused to end.</li><li>Its 1836 constitution made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> a cornerstone of the state: slaves could not be freed without the consent of Congress, Congress could pass no law against slavery or the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">slave trade</a>, and no free person of African descent could reside permanently in the republic.</li><li>Only seven nations recognised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/texas">Texas</a>, and its claim to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-grande">Rio Grande</a> as its border, disputed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> throughout, passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> at annexation and led to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexican-american-war">Mexican–American War</a>.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>In 1839 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/texas">Texas</a> became the first nation in the world to enact a homestead exemption, under which creditors cannot seize a person's primary residence.</p></aside>
    </section>

