    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/stephen-f-austin"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/stephen-f-austin.jpg" alt="Stephen F. Austin" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/stephen-f-austin">Stephen F. Austin</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Stephen_Fuller_Austin_Posthumous_Portrait.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/texas">Texas</a> had been a thinly settled province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, where Anglo-American filibusters proclaimed short-lived republics in 1813 and 1819. After Mexican independence in 1821 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stephen-f-austin">Stephen F. Austin</a> led the Old Three Hundred into the province and other empresarios followed; in 1824 Texas was joined with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coahuila">Coahuila</a> as the state of Coahuila y Tejas. High <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> prices and the success of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mississippi-river">Mississippi</a> plantations drew large numbers of white Americans with slaves to copy that model. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> abolished <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> in 1829, but the Texians refused to end it, replacing it with long-term indenture contracts signed by "liberated" slaves, and the Law of April 6, 1830 outlawed American immigration. The empresarios' Convention of 1832 was the first formal step towards revolution.</p><p>By 1834 American immigrants greatly outnumbered Mexicans. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antonio-lopez-de-santa-anna">Antonio López de Santa Anna</a>'s regime adopted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siete-leyes">Siete Leyes</a>, abolishing the authority of the states, and began enforcing the anti-slavery laws, the settlers objected to mandatory <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholicism">Catholicism</a> and feared worse. In 1835 the central government split Coahuila y Tejas in two; fighting opened at the Battle of Gonzales on October 2, 1835, and a provisional government, the Consultation, formed in November. The Convention of 1836 met on March 1 and declared independence the next day. David G. Burnet became interim president with Lorenzo de Zavala as vice-president; major fighting ended on April 21, 1836, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sam-houston">Sam Houston</a> was elected president on September 5.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/cotton"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cotton.jpg" alt="Cotton" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/cotton">Cotton</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cotton_-_%E0%B4%AA%E0%B4%B0%E0%B5%81%E0%B4%A4%E0%B5%8D%E0%B4%A4%E0%B4%BF_03.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">കാക്കര, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The republic's economy rested on farming and on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a>, a cornerstone of the Texian economy, above all in agriculture. Its main exports were crops, chiefly <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> and corn; it imported iron, machinery and goods such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tea">tea</a>, beer and chocolate from the nations that recognised it, and small amounts went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>. Settlers from the southern states brought slavery with them; it fuelled the economy and reinforced social inequality. The Constitution of 1836 wrote it into law: African slaves brought to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/texas">Texas</a> remained slaves and could not be emancipated without the consent of Congress, Congress could make no law restricting slavery or the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">slave trade</a>, and no free person of African descent, in whole or in part, might reside permanently in the republic without that consent. Texans of Mexican descent were Tejanos, the Anglo-Americans Texians; women worked in domestic labour, agriculture and business with very limited legal rights.</p><p>Public finance was weak: there was no central bank, the Texas dollar was volatile and private banking largely unregulated. The cotton price crash of the 1840s bankrupted the republic, and its government's huge debts became a motive for annexation. Congress nevertheless chartered colleges, from the University of San Augustine in 1837 to Baylor University in 1845. In 1839 Texas became the first nation to enact a homestead exemption protecting a person's primary residence from creditors.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/mirabeau-b-lamar"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mirabeau-b-lamar.jpg" alt="Mirabeau B. Lamar" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/mirabeau-b-lamar">Mirabeau B. Lamar</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mirabeaulamar_2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author; derivative work by Oldag07, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The republic was never at peace. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/comanche">Comanches</a>, whose territory included north-west <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/texas">Texas</a>, were its main Indigenous opponents. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sam-houston">Sam Houston</a> negotiated peace with them in the late 1830s, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mirabeau-b-lamar">Mirabeau B. Lamar</a> reversed that policy in 1838 and ordered an invasion of Comancheria. After peace talks in 1840 ended with the massacre of 34 Comanche leaders in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-antonio">San Antonio</a>, Potsanaquahip (Buffalo Hump) led 500 to 700 Comanche cavalry down the Guadalupe valley to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-mexico">Gulf of Mexico</a>, sacking Victoria and Linnville in the Great Raid of 1840; pursued by 186 rangers, they lost their plunder at the Battle of Plum Creek. Houston, president again in 1841, made a new peace with both sides exhausted; the Treaty of Tehuacana Creek of 1844, signed without the Comanches, could not be enforced for want of men and money.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> refused to recognise independence. Lamar's unofficial Santa Fe Expedition of 1841, 320 soldiers with merchants, aimed at eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-mexico">New Mexico</a>; its members were captured, and two raids on the Santa Fe Trail in 1843 also failed. On March 5, 1842 Ráfael Vásquez's 500 men briefly occupied San Antonio, and on September 11 Adrián Woll's 1,400 captured it; Texans fought them at the Battle of Salado Creek while, a mile and a half away, Mexican soldiers massacred 53 volunteers who had surrendered. At sea the Texas Navy's Austin and Wharton under Commodore Edwin Ward Moore fought the Mexican steamers inconclusively at the Naval Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/campeche">Campeche</a> in 1843 beside Yucatán.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Constitution of 1836 made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/texas">Texas</a> a centralised unitary state, unlike the federal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>. In September 1836 the republic elected a Congress of 14 senators and 29 representatives; the first president served two years, later ones three, and only male citizens could vote or hold office. A Supreme Court had a chief justice appointed by the president, James Collinsworth the first, and four associates elected by both houses for four-year terms, who also presided over four judicial districts; each of the 23 counties had its own court, sheriff, coroner and justices of the peace. Five sites served as capitals in 1836 before Houston in 1837 and, under Lamar in 1839, the new town of Austin, confirmed as the seat of government by the Archive War, when the archives Houston had removed were returned at gunpoint.</p><p>Citizenship followed race. Section 10 made all residents on the day of independence citizens except "Africans, the descendants of Africans, and Indians"; white immigrants qualified after six months and an oath of loyalty. Politics turned on two factions: the War Party under Lamar, for independence, the expulsion of the Native Americans and expansion to the Pacific, and the Peace Party under Houston, for annexation and coexistence. Congress even claimed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-californias">The Californias</a> over Houston's veto. The 1844 election split the republic, the newer western regions for Edward Burleson and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> country east of the Trinity River for Anson Jones.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/andrew-jackson"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/andrew-jackson.jpg" alt="Andrew Jackson" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/andrew-jackson">Andrew Jackson</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Andrew_jackson_head_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Ralph Eleaser Whiteside Earl, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/texas">Texas</a>'s status as a slaveholding country and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>'s claim on its territory shaped all its foreign relations. Only seven nations recognised it: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> on March 3, 1837, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andrew-jackson">Andrew Jackson</a> appointed Alcée La Branche chargé d'affaires; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> on September 25, 1839, whose Legation of 1841 still stands in Austin; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a>, which exchanged recognition for free trade, the Republic of Yucatán, and the United Kingdom, which admitted Texian goods while deferring to Mexico and recognised the republic in the 1840s, after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> crash, in a failed attempt to coerce it into giving up <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> and to check American expansion.</p><p>Though most Texians wanted annexation from the start, both Democrats and Whigs in Washington resisted adding a vast slaveholding region to a country already divided over slavery, and feared war with Mexico. The republic's claims outran its reach: it took the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-grande">Rio Grande</a> as its boundary, including eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-mexico">New Mexico</a>, which Mexico administered throughout, and hoped for a railroad to the Gulf of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/california">California</a>; much of the disputed land was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/comanche">Comanche</a> country outside either state's control, and some districts sent representatives to both congresses. Those claims, inherited by the United States, led to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexican-american-war">Mexican–American War</a>. The Naval Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/campeche">Campeche</a> is engraved on the cylinder of every Colt 1851 Navy, 1860 Army and 1861 Navy revolver.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/mexican-american-war"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mexican-american-war.jpg" alt="Mexican–American War" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/mexican-american-war">Mexican–American War</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nebel_Mexican_War_12_Scott_in_Mexico_City_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Adolphe Jean-Baptiste Bayot / Carl Nebel, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> price crash of the 1840s bankrupted the republic and made foreign protection against a Mexican reconquest urgent, while its debts argued for annexation. On February 28, 1845 the U.S. Congress passed a bill authorising it, which President John Tyler signed on March 1, with the date set for December 29. The British and French ministers hurried to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico-city">Mexico City</a>, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> signed a "Diplomatic Act" offering to recognise an independent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/texas">Texas</a> with mediated boundaries. President Anson Jones sent both offers to a specially elected convention at Austin, which accepted the American proposal with one dissenting vote; the Mexican offer was never put to a vote.</p><p>On October 13, 1845 a large majority of voters approved annexation and a state constitution that specifically endorsed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> and the bringing of slaves into Texas. Congress accepted it, and Texas became a state on December 29, 1845, bypassing any territorial phase; the republic formally handed over power on February 19, 1846. The joint resolution allowed up to four more states to be made from its territory and let Texas keep its public lands, so that the state later controlled its oil reserves. In the Compromise of 1850 Texas sold its claims beyond its present borders for $10,000,000 in federal bonds. The border dispute with Mexico, which had refused to sell or to recognise, passed to Washington and brought on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexican-american-war">Mexican–American War</a>.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/texas">Texas</a>, declared by Anglo-American settlers on 2 March 1836 after Santa Anna centralised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> and moved against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a>, wrote slavery and white citizenship into its constitution, fought Mexico and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/comanche">Comanches</a> for nine years, won recognition from seven nations and, bankrupt after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> crash, joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> on 29 December 1845.</em></p>

